<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><!-- Original-type: ra--><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Br J Cancer</journal-id><journal-title>British Journal of Cancer</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0007-0920</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1532-1827</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Nature Publishing Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">10574266</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2362905</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">6690758</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/sj.bjc.6690758</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Regular Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Both cell proliferation and apoptosis significantly predict shortened disease-free survival in hepatocellular carcinoma</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ito</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Matsuura</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff2">2</xref><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Sakon</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Takeda</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Umeshita</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Nagano</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Nakamori</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Dono</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Tsujimoto</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">3</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Nakahara</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">4</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Nakao</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff3">4</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Monden</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1">1</xref></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="aff1"><label>1</label>Department of Surgery II, Osaka University Medical School, Osaka, Japan</aff><aff id="aff2"><label>2</label>Department of Pathology, School of Allied Health Science, Faculty of Medicine, Osaka University, Osaka, Japan</aff><aff id="aff3"><label>3</label>Departments of Pathology, <label>4</label>Surgery, Osaka Police Hospital, Osaka, Japan</aff><author-notes><corresp id="caf1"><label>*</label>Author for correspondence: </corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><month>10</month><year>1999</year></pub-date><volume>81</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>747</fpage><lpage>751</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>20</day><month>10</month><year>1998</year></date><date date-type="rev-recd"><day>09</day><month>02</month><year>1999</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>16</day><month>02</month><year>1999</year></date></history><copyright-statement>Copyright 1999, Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1999</copyright-year><permissions><copyright-holder>Cancer Research Campaign</copyright-holder></permissions><abstract><p>In this study, we investigated the proliferating cell index by the percentage of Ki-67 expressing cells (Ki-67 LI) and the apoptotic index (AI) by the number of morphologically apoptotic cells per 1000 carcinoma cells in haematoxylin and eosin sections of 76 hepatocellular carcinomas (HCC). Both indices showed excellent correlation with each other (<italic>P</italic> &#x0003c; 0.0001) and were significantly higher in cases of poor differentiation, of advanced stages, with portal invasion and with intrahepatic metastasis. Furthermore, cases with higher Ki-67 LI or higher AI displayed poor outcomes for disease-free survival (<italic>P</italic> = 0.0001 and <italic>P</italic> = 0.0005) by univariate analysis. By multivariate analysis, both indices could be regarded as independent prognostic factors. These results strongly suggest that Ki-67 LI and AI have very similar clinical significance, reflecting the existence of biologically aggressive phenotypes and poor disease-free survival rate in HCC. &#x000a9; 1999 Cancer Research Campaign</p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>Ki-67</kwd><kwd>apoptosis</kwd><kwd>immunohistochemistry</kwd><kwd>hepatocellular carcinoma</kwd><kwd>prognostic factor</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>